                    coach_default:          `Terms logged.`,
                    coach_r1_interest_right:`<strong>That is the interest behind the position.</strong> Voltaic's push for majority is really fear of its cell IP leaking — the mirror image of your 2019 wound. Now you can offer it IP protection through governance instead of handing over control of the whole venture.`,
                    coach_r1_majority:      `<strong>You read the position, not the interest.</strong> Giving Voltaic 60% “because it wants majority” surrenders the very control you need. What is the <em>interest</em> underneath that demand? It is IP protection — and you can satisfy that without losing the venture.`,
                    coach_r1_cash:          `<strong>Cash is not the live wire here.</strong> Both sides care about returns, but the negotiation that defines the next decade is about control and IP, not the dividend split. Diagnose what each party must <em>protect</em>, not just what it earns.`,
                    coach_r1_board:         `<strong>Counting seats is positional thinking.</strong> Matching board seats to equity feels tidy, but it misses the interest underneath: Voltaic fears IP leakage and you fear a repeat of 2019. Design around those interests, not the seat count.`,
                    coach_r1_fullvalue:     `<strong>Good — you are not negotiating against yourself.</strong> Valuing the software team and regulatory access near the top of the range gives you a real basis to claim more than book contribution. Just be ready to justify it; pushing the very top costs a little trust up front.`,
                    coach_r1_underprice:    `<strong>You just rounded yourself down.</strong> Pricing Sundara's contribution near $90M to keep things moving hands Voltaic value capture for free — your distribution, regulatory access and software team are worth far more. Was the deadline pressure real, or did you negotiate against yourself?`,
                    coach_r1_pricing:       `<strong>Pricing control is not where 2019 lives.</strong> Day-to-day pricing matters operationally, but the decision that could repeat your wound is who may use the shared roadmap and IP. Aim your control there.`,
                    coach_r1_ceo:           `<strong>The CEO matters, but it is not the 2019 risk.</strong> Fighting hardest for your own appointee is reasonable, yet the decision that could recreate appropriation is IP licensing back to the parents. Protect that first.`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You named Voltaic's true interest as IP protection AND identified IP licensing back to the parents as the decision where losing control repeats 2019. That is exactly the map that lets you trade equity for the rights that actually matter.`,
                    coach_r2_equity51:      `<strong>You fought for 51% — and paid for it in trust and capital.</strong> A nominal majority feels like control, but unless you also win the reserved matters on IP licensing, Voltaic still steers the technology roadmap inside the JV. Is 51% of the equity worth less control than 49% with veto rights would have been?`,
                    coach_r2_minority:      `<strong>Taking the protected-minority structure is the sophisticated play.</strong> 49% with the right reserved matters and a deadlock mechanism can out-protect a nominal 51% owner — and it costs Voltaic far less trust than grabbing the bigger slice.`,
                    coach_r2_5050:          `<strong>Fifty-fifty feels fair</strong> — and it can work, but only if you build a credible deadlock mechanism. Equal ownership without a tie-breaker is not balance; it is a stalemate waiting for the first disagreement.`,
                    coach_r2_4060:          `<strong>You gave Voltaic a clear majority to close fast.</strong> That buys speed and goodwill, but unless your reserved matters and IP ring-fence are strong, you are now a junior partner in a venture built on your market and your software. Control has to come from governance now.`,
                    coach_r2_reserved_sweet:`<strong>This is control without majority.</strong> A targeted set of reserved matters on the decisions that matter — IP licensing, change of scope — protects Sundara as a minority far more than a bigger equity slice would. That is the equity-versus-rights lesson in action.`,
                    coach_r2_reserved_heavy:`<strong>Too many reserved matters is deadlock by design.</strong> Requiring both partners' consent on almost everything protects you on paper but freezes the JV the first time you disagree — and Voltaic reads the demand as distrust. Reserve the few that matter, not everything.`,
                    coach_r2_reserved_thin: `<strong>Thin reserved matters leave you exposed.</strong> Without veto rights on the decisions that matter, your equity share is just economics — Voltaic can still steer the roadmap. Reserved matters, not the equity number, are where minority protection lives.`,
                    coach_r2_ringfence:     `<strong>You ring-fenced the crown jewel.</strong> Licensing the software IP in with clawback — rather than transferring it — means a breakup cannot hand your know-how to a co-owner. That is the structural answer to 2019, paired with reserved matters on licensing.`,
                    coach_r2_transfer:      `<strong>That is the 2019 mistake with extra steps.</strong> Transfer the BMS/software IP into EV-Cell India and it sits in a venture Voltaic co-owns and could access on a breakup. Why hand over the crown jewel when you could license it in, keep ownership, and claw it back if the partnership ends?`,
                    coach_r2_deadlock5050:  `<strong>A 50/50 with no tie-breaker is a frozen JV.</strong> In year two you and Voltaic disagree on whether to cut prices to defend share, neither side can decide, and the market moves without you. Equal equity needs a credible deadlock mechanism or it designs in paralysis.`,
                    coach_r3_targeted:      `<strong>A targeted exit package is the disciplined choice.</strong> A sensible lock-in, a right of first refusal and a buy-sell give you real protection against a repeat of 2019 without the trust cost of stacking every clause you can imagine.`,
                    coach_r3_stacked:       `<strong>You demanded everything — and partner trust just collapsed.</strong> A 7-year lock-in, a shotgun, a non-compete, clawback AND change-of-control triggers. Voltaic's counsel is asking whether you want a partner or a hostage. Which two clauses genuinely prevent a repeat of 2019, and which are just fear killing the deal?`,
                    coach_r3_light:         `<strong>Maximum goodwill, minimum protection.</strong> A short lock-in alone signals trust, but it leaves the 2019 risk wide open — nothing stops Voltaic flying early or your roadmap leaking. You can protect yourself with a few clauses without poisoning the relationship.`,
                    coach_r3_longlock:      `<strong>A long lock-in reads as distrust.</strong> Beyond about five years, the lock-in stops looking like prudent stability and starts telling Voltaic you expect a divorce. It also costs you flexibility and trust for a protection a shorter term already gives you.`,
                    coach_r3_nolock:        `<strong>No lock-in invites early flight.</strong> Without a lock-in either side can sell out the moment it suits them — exactly the instability a JV at formation should design against. A modest lock-in is protection, not aggression.`,
                    coach_r3_clawback:      `<strong>Clawback closes the 2019 wound on exit.</strong> Pairing IP clawback with a focused non-compete means that if the partnership ends, your know-how returns to you and cannot be turned into a competing product. That is structural protection, not goodwill.`,
                    coach_r3_sharedip:      `<strong>Jointly owned IP on a breakup is the 2019 trap.</strong> If the software IP stays co-owned, a divorce leaves Voltaic holding your crown jewel — precisely how a partner launched against you before. Claw it back instead of sharing it.`,
                    coach_r3_nothingip:     `<strong>“The general clauses will cover it” is how 2019 happened.</strong> Leave the fate of the shared roadmap unspecified and you are trusting goodwill with the one asset you cannot afford to lose. Specify clawback explicitly.`,
                    coach_r3_nopacing:      `<strong>Demands dumped all at once read as bad faith.</strong> Even reasonable clauses, fired as a list with no sequencing or justification, spend trust faster than they need to. Pace and explain your protections and Voltaic can accept them.`,
                    coach_r4_quantify:      `<strong>You priced the threat before responding.</strong> A time-limited royalty costs a known number per year; Voltaic walking costs your rival the cell tech and 3–4 share points — a far larger loss. Now you know whether to concede or hold, instead of folding on fear.`,
                    coach_r4_cave:          `<strong>You folded without the arithmetic.</strong> Conceding the royalty because the rival threat felt dangerous may have cost more than the walk ever would. Did you price it? If the concession is bigger than the loss from Voltaic walking, you negotiated against yourself under pressure.`,
                    coach_r4_holdall:       `<strong>Holding firm on everything dares Voltaic to walk.</strong> Your terms may be fair, but refusing to flex anything at the wire — when the rival is genuinely courting Voltaic — risks the existential loss of the cell tech going to your competitor. Decide what is truly non-negotiable, not all of it.`,
                    coach_r4_flexroyalty:   `<strong>Flex the royalty, protect the structure.</strong> A time-limited royalty is the right thing to give: it costs a quantifiable amount and buys the close, while your IP ring-fence and reserved matters — the things that prevent 2019 — stay untouchable.`,
                    coach_r4_flexreserved:  `<strong>Giving up reserved matters for speed is the wrong trade.</strong> The reserved matters are where your control lives. Surrender them to keep the cash terms and you have a richer slice of a venture you can no longer steer — the equity-versus-control error at the worst moment.`,
                    coach_r4_flexip:        `<strong>Dropping the IP clawback to close recreates 2019.</strong> The clawback is precisely the clause that prevents a repeat of your wound. Trade it away under deadline pressure and you have closed a deal that leaves your crown jewel exposed.`,
                    coach_r4_gohonest:      `<strong>A board-ready close.</strong> You priced the walk-away, flexed only a time-limited royalty, protected the IP ring-fence and reserved matters, and presented binding heads of agreement with the rationale. That is value capture and protection held together — the architect's close.`,
                    coach_r4_hold:          `<strong>Refusing to close until Voltaic fully withdraws is brinkmanship.</strong> With the rival genuinely in the wings, an all-or-nothing hold risks the deal you spent four rounds building. A priced, partial concession would have closed it without surrendering what matters.`,

                    sum_default:            `Terms set.`,
