                    sum_r3_over:            `Over-stacked clauses — partner trust collapsing.`,
                    sum_r3_weak:            `Exit terms under- or over-protective.`,
                    sum_r4_good:            `Priced the walk-away, flexed the royalty, closed honestly.`,
                    sum_r4_weak:            `Caved, over-held, or traded away the protection that mattered.`,

                    insight_master:         `Master architect: you closed the deal with control via reserved matters, the IP structurally ring-fenced, fair value capture from the $300M pie, and partner trust intact. You separated equity from control, designed the exit at formation, and priced the day-50 walk-away — the whole alliance-design playbook executed.`,
                    insight_walked:         `Voltaic walked to your rival. Your protective demands spent partner trust below the level at which Voltaic believed you wanted a partner at all — or you held all-or-nothing at the wire. Your rival now gets the cell tech and 3–4 points of your market share over two years. Protection that kills the deal protects nothing.`,
                    insight_exposed:        `The deal closed, but Sundara is exposed. You hold equity and a signature, yet your real decision rights or your IP ring-fence are thin — the kind of structure where the 2019 wound can reopen. Equity and a closed deal are not the same as control and protection.`,
                    insight_thin:           `The deal closed but the architecture is thin. Re-examine whether your control came from reserved matters or just an equity number, and whether the software IP is structurally protected or merely covered by goodwill.`,
                    insight_ipweak:         `IP protection stayed low — the crown jewel is not structurally safe. Licensing in with clawback and a non-compete on exit, rather than transferring or sharing the IP, is what closes the 2019 risk.`,
                    insight_controlweak:    `Control is weak — you optimised for the equity number rather than the decision rights. A 49% partner with strong reserved matters and a deadlock mechanism would have been better protected than the slice you chose.`,
                    insight_trustgone:      `Partner trust fell dangerously low. Every protective clause is information about what you fear; demanded all at once they read as bad faith. Sequencing and justifying demands is part of the design, not an afterthought.`,
                    insight_overreach:      `You over-reached on protection and capital. Stacking clauses or reserving almost every decision protects you on paper but spends the trust and goodwill the JV runs on — and risks deadlock or a walk.`,
                    insight_balanced:       `You balanced control against collaboration: protected on the few decisions that matter, ring-fenced the IP, and kept enough trust for Voltaic to want the partnership. That is the central design problem solved.`,

                    hint_trust:             `Partner trust is dangerously low — Voltaic is starting to doubt you want a partner. Pace your demands, justify each protective clause, and drop the ones that are fear rather than real protection.`,
                    hint_overreach:         `You are over-reaching — too many clauses or reserved matters for too little marginal protection. Keep the two or three that genuinely prevent a repeat of 2019 and release the rest to recover trust.`,
                    hint_ip:                `IP protection is weak — the crown jewel is exposed. License the software IP in with clawback rather than transferring or sharing it, so a breakup cannot hand it to Voltaic.`,
                    hint_control:           `Control is thin. Real protection comes from reserved matters and a deadlock mechanism on the decisions that matter — not from the equity percentage alone.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong architecture — control, IP protection and trust are all healthy. Hold this balance through the close and price the day-50 walk-away rather than folding on fear.`,

